"Phlimm" <icubed@deathtospamremovethisbigfoot.com> wrote:

> Is there an "ethical" limit to the degree of
> horribleness of a bones file that one uploads to
> the Hearse?  In other words, if I died in quite a
> nasty and for all intents and purposes impossible
> to beat situation, should I not upload it?

No, I think the "ethics" are two fold: first, don't,
as it says in the FAQ, upload bones files that are
the result of scum saving (or, I suppose bones
recycling); those are _artificially_ nasty.

Second: but otherwise, bad things and good things
both happen to good people, so if you are going to
participate in Hearse, don't pick and choose what to
upload, upload them all, so as not to artificially
bias the stockpile with your personal inclinations.
That isn't what happens on a multiuser site, that
isn't what should happen using Hearse to emulate
one.

Remember, there is always a chance dying on a bones
level won't _drop_ a bones file, so the recipient of
an impossible-to-beat one (a) will only see it once,
then it goes back to the bone yard to be someone
elses problem, (b) may be the one at whose machine
the bones file dies from not being dropped again, or
(c) may be that exceptional player who goes to
Hearse for the very purpose of getting "impossible"
to solve bones files, and _beats_ the monstrosity,
thus getting a really good experience out of your
misfortune!

